{"causes": ["The intellectual flourishing of the School of Isfahan under Safavid patronage created a fertile ground for philosophical and theological innovation.", "The groundbreaking philosophical synthesis developed by Mullā Ṣadrā provided a complex system that required systematization and popularization.", "The prevailing intellectual climate in 17th-century Iran encouraged the integration of rational sciences, mystical thought, and Shiʿi theology."], "effects": ["Abd al-Razzaq Lahiji's work helped to systematically transmit and popularize Mullā Ṣadrā's transcendent philosophy across Safavid Iran.", "His philosophical treatises became standard texts in Iranian madrasas, shaping the curriculum for centuries of Shiʿi religious education.", "He solidified the intellectual legacy of the School of Isfahan, ensuring the continuity of its unique synthesis of philosophical and mystical traditions."], "relationships": [{"sourceSlug": "mulla-sadra", "sourceName": "Mullā Ṣadrā", "verb": "INFLUENCES", "targetSlug": "abd-al-razzaq-lahiji", "targetName": "Abd al-Razzaq Lahiji", "context": "Mullā Ṣadrā profoundly influenced Abd al-Razzaq Lahiji as his primary philosophical mentor and father-in-law in the early 17th century."}, {"sourceSlug": "abd-al-razzaq-lahiji", "sourceName": "Abd al-Razzaq Lahiji", "verb": "TRANSMITS", "targetSlug": "school-of-isfahan", "targetName": "School of Isfahan", "context": "Abd al-Razzaq Lahiji transmitted the philosophical doctrines of the School of Isfahan through his writings and teachings in the mid-17th century."}, {"sourceSlug": "abd-al-razzaq-lahiji", "sourceName": "Abd al-Razzaq Lahiji", "verb": "CREATES", "targetSlug": "gawhar-e-morad", "targetName": "Gawhar-e Morād", "context": "Abd al-Razzaq Lahiji authored "Gawhar-e Morād" (The Desired Jewel) around 1640, a comprehensive treatise on metaphysics and theology."}, {"sourceSlug": "gawhar-e-morad", "sourceName": "Gawhar-e Morād", "verb": "INFLUENCES", "targetSlug": "islamic-philosophy", "targetName": "Islamic Philosophy", "context": "Lahiji's "Gawhar-e Morād" significantly influenced later Islamic philosophy by popularizing Sadra's complex ideas throughout the Safavid era and beyond."}, {"sourceSlug": "abd-al-razzaq-lahiji", "sourceName": "Abd al-Razzaq Lahiji", "verb": "OCCURS_IN", "targetSlug": "qom-iran", "targetName": "Qom, Iran", "context": "Abd al-Razzaq Lahiji taught at the Madrasa-ye ʿAbdallāh Shushtarī in Qom, a major center of Shiʿi learning, until his death in 1662."}], "places": [{"name": "Isfahan, Iran", "role": "Intellectual Center"}, {"name": "Qom, Iran", "role": "Teaching Location"}, {"name": "Lahijan, Iran", "role": "Origin of Nisba"}], "texts": [{"title": "Wikipedia: Abd al-Razzaq Lahiji", "type": "Reference article", "slug": "abd-al-razzaq-lahiji"}], "externalLinks": ["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q3943957", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Abd_al-Razzaq_Lahiji"], "tags": ["Philosophers & Thinkers", "doctrine-development", "cause-and-effect", "temporal-linkage"], "thumbnailUrl": "", "quote": "", "legacySummary": "", "historicalSignificance": {"significanceScore": 7, "significanceNarrative": "Abd al-Razzaq Lahiji was pivotal in systematizing and transmitting the complex philosophical legacy of Mullā Ṣadrā, ensuring its enduring influence on Shiʿi thought and education for centuries. His works became foundational texts in Iranian madrasas, shaping the intellectual landscape of the region.", "significanceCategory": "regional"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-07-17T06:56:16Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#29561384118", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "ʿAbd-Al-Razzāq B. ʿAlī B. Al-Hosayn Lāhījī was an Iranian theologian, poet and philosopher. His mentor in philosophy was his father-in-law Mulla Sadra.", "newValue": "ʿAbd-al-Razzāq b. ʿAlī b. al-Ḥosayn Lāhījī (died 1662), known by his pen name Fayyāż ("the Giver of Abundance"), was a towering figure in 17th-century Safavid Iran, a polymath whose intellectual contributions spanned theology, philosophy, and poetry. As a prominent disciple and son-in-law of the seminal philosopher Mullā Ṣadrā, Lāhījī became a leading exponent of the School of Isfahan, solidifying and transmitting the complex metaphysical doctrines of his mentor. His work was crucial in integrating rational philosophy (ḥikmah) with mystical intuition (ʿirfān) and Shiʿi theology, thereby shaping the intellectual landscape of Persianate Islam for centuries.

Lāhījī's philosophical output, primarily written in Persian and Arabic, sought to systematize and popularize Mullā Ṣadrā's transcendent philosophy (al-ḥikma al-mutaʿāliya). His magnum opus, "Gawhar-e Morād" (The Desired Jewel), composed around 1640, is a comprehensive treatise on metaphysics, theology, ethics, and eschatology, serving as a key text for understanding Sadra's thought and earning him the epithet "Ṣadr-e Thānī" (The Second Ṣadr). Beyond philosophy, he was a respected poet, leaving behind a Dīwān that further showcased his erudition and spiritual depth.

His role as a teacher at the Madrasa-ye ʿAbdallāh Shushtarī in Qom was pivotal in institutionalizing and disseminating the philosophical insights of the School of Isfahan. Through his detailed commentaries and accessible summaries of Sadra's challenging ideas, Lāhījī ensured that Sadra's unique synthesis of Peripatetic, Illuminationist, and mystical traditions became a cornerstone of advanced religious and philosophical education in Iran. His clear expositions clarified complex concepts such as the substantial motion (ḥarakat-e jawharī) and the unity of existence (waḥdat al-wujūd), making them accessible to a wider scholarly audience."}, {"timestamp": "2026-07-17T06:56:16Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#29561384118", "field": "detailsJson.causes", "oldValue": [{"title": "Classical era: flourishing of philosophical inquiry, imperial expansion, and codification of la", "type": "EventWindow", "year": "Classical"}, {"title": "Ongoing philosophical inquiry and intellectual debate", "type": "Idea", "year": ""}], "newValue": ["The intellectual flourishing of the School of Isfahan under Safavid patronage created a fertile ground for philosophical and theological innovation.", "The groundbreaking philosophical synthesis developed by Mullā Ṣadrā provided a complex system that required systematization and popularization.", "The prevailing intellectual climate in 17th-century Iran encouraged the integration of rational sciences, mystical thought, and Shiʿi theology."]}, {"timestamp": "2026-07-17T06:56:16Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#29561384118", "field": "detailsJson.effects", "oldValue": [{"title": "Contributed philosophical ideas to ongoing intellectual discourse", "type": "Idea", "year": "1662"}, {"title": "Contributed to enduring intellectual and institutional frameworks that shaped subsequent civili", "type": "Movement", "year": "1662"}], "newValue": ["Abd al-Razzaq Lahiji's work helped to systematically transmit and popularize Mullā Ṣadrā's transce